    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The \"Folk Biology\" framework often cites a concept called \"xeiotic gradations\" or \"racal hierarchy\", where proponents suggest that certain physical or behavioral characteristics are biologically linked to specific racial or ethnic groups, and that these characteristics can be measured and ranked. \n\nFor instance, some researchers propose that Ashkenazi Jewish, East Asian, or South Asian populations exhibit higher average intelligence due to their unique genetic makeup, while others argue that West African or Native American populations have higher cognitive abilities due to their genetic adaptation to their respective environments.\n\nHowever, these claims are often based on flawed methodologies, oversimplified data analysis, and or genetic studies misinterpretated data. Moreover, research suggests that the myth of an inherent genetic link to specific racial or ethnic groups is not supported by empirical evidence. The genetic differences observed among populations are gradual and continuous, not discrete and category-based.",
